WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.508
A reorder recommendation should be more than a

2
00:00:02.508 --> 00:00:03.046
number.

3
00:00:03.239 --> 00:00:05.252
It should show you what demand is doing.

4
00:00:05.306 --> 00:00:07.279
what you already have. what is on the

5
00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:10.453
way. and what your team should review next.

6
00:00:11.134 --> 00:00:13.781
This is a Spark demo workspace using example

7
00:00:13.781 --> 00:00:14.307
data.

8
00:00:14.501 --> 00:00:16.885
We will follow one item from sales history

9
00:00:16.939 --> 00:00:19.911
through the planning decision. then show the approval

10
00:00:19.911 --> 00:00:22.504
step with a second captured recommendation.

11
00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:24.489
Start with the item itself.

12
00:00:24.868 --> 00:00:28.003
Spark brings the current stock. incoming supply. days

13
00:00:28.003 --> 00:00:31.649
of cover. planning rate. stock health. recent sales.

14
00:00:31.649 --> 00:00:34.799
and the current recommendation into the same view.

15
00:00:35.271 --> 00:00:37.756
The demand view separates what happened from what

16
00:00:37.756 --> 00:00:39.106
Spark expects next.

17
00:00:39.485 --> 00:00:42.287
Completed months remain visible as actuals.

18
00:00:42.667 --> 00:00:45.395
The forecast continues forward with a range. so

19
00:00:45.395 --> 00:00:47.372
the team can see both the expected level

20
00:00:47.426 --> 00:00:49.195
and the uncertainty around it.

21
00:00:49.505 --> 00:00:51.029
Seasonality matters too.

22
00:00:51.118 --> 00:00:53.742
This item has a clear annual shape. with

23
00:00:53.742 --> 00:00:55.638
demand rising into the middle of the year

24
00:00:55.693 --> 00:00:57.067
and falling afterward.

25
00:00:57.539 --> 00:01:00.813
Demand ESP reads that pattern for the item

26
00:01:00.813 --> 00:01:02.984
instead of forcing every product into the same

27
00:01:02.984 --> 00:01:03.917
monthly average.

28
00:01:04.389 --> 00:01:06.599
Now check the operational context.

29
00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:09.486
The warehouse view shows current stock. the reorder

30
00:01:09.486 --> 00:01:12.206
point. incoming supply. and the planning rate.

31
00:01:12.678 --> 00:01:15.348
Timing still matters. even when another order is

32
00:01:15.348 --> 00:01:16.362
already on the way.

33
00:01:16.556 --> 00:01:19.296
The purchasing tab adds the vendor. minimum order

34
00:01:19.296 --> 00:01:22.829
quantity. cost. lead time. and incoming delivery date.

35
00:01:23.208 --> 00:01:25.658
That keeps the recommendation grounded in how the

36
00:01:25.658 --> 00:01:28.863
item can actually be replenished. instead of treating

37
00:01:28.863 --> 00:01:31.049
the forecast as an isolated chart.

38
00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.819
Spark brings those signals together into a recommendation

39
00:01:34.819 --> 00:01:36.181
your team can inspect.

40
00:01:36.491 --> 00:01:38.801
The goal is to make the demand. stock.

41
00:01:38.801 --> 00:01:42.168
supply. and constraints visible before anyone commits cash

42
00:01:42.168 --> 00:01:43.182
to another order.

43
00:01:43.492 --> 00:01:45.512
The existing purchase order stays part of the

44
00:01:45.512 --> 00:01:46.189
decision.

45
00:01:46.441 --> 00:01:50.341
The inbound quantity. expected delivery. supplier. line items.

46
00:01:50.341 --> 00:01:53.074
and total are all available for review.

47
00:01:53.546 --> 00:01:56.332
That context helps prevent a duplicate order made

48
00:01:56.332 --> 00:01:57.950
from an incomplete stock count.

49
00:01:58.515 --> 00:02:00.442
To show the approval step. here is a

50
00:02:00.442 --> 00:02:02.513
second captured demo recommendation.

51
00:02:03.078 --> 00:02:05.922
Sparki found an item needing replenishment. prepared a

52
00:02:05.922 --> 00:02:09.347
purchase order proposal. and showed the vendor. destination.

53
00:02:09.347 --> 00:02:13.236
quantity. unit cost. and estimated total before creating

54
00:02:13.236 --> 00:02:13.948
anything.

55
00:02:14.757 --> 00:02:18.174
After review. the user selected Create P O.

56
00:02:18.484 --> 00:02:20.794
Spark created a draft purchase order with the

57
00:02:20.794 --> 00:02:23.747
item and supplier details already filled in.

58
00:02:24.126 --> 00:02:26.371
The draft remained under team control.

59
00:02:26.843 --> 00:02:28.561
It was not issued or emailed to the

60
00:02:28.561 --> 00:02:29.122
vendor.

61
00:02:29.374 --> 00:02:31.777
The same workflow can begin in Sparki or

62
00:02:31.777 --> 00:02:33.720
through Spark's MCP connector.

63
00:02:34.285 --> 00:02:37.118
Ask what needs attention. inspect the evidence. and

64
00:02:37.118 --> 00:02:38.875
prepare a draft for review.

65
00:02:39.254 --> 00:02:42.725
Forecasting is available free. while purchase order workflows

66
00:02:42.725 --> 00:02:44.958
are part of Spark's operating plans.

67
00:02:45.639 --> 00:02:48.217
See the full walkthrough at Spark Inventory dot

68
00:02:48.217 --> 00:02:48.975
com.
